/**
@defgroup unit_tests Unit tests
@brief CMocka unit tests for the LAME library and the frontend.

The tests in @c test/unit test single functions on their own, with the
[CMocka](https://cmocka.org/) framework. They are optional. The build compiles
and runs them only when it is configured with

@code
./configure --enable-unit-tests
@endcode

The default is off. With this option, @c "make check" builds and runs every
test in @c test/unit and reports for each test whether it passed. The tests use
only the CMocka macros from before version 2.0, because cmocka 2.0 is not yet
available in many distributions.

@section unit_tests_running Running the tests

@code
./configure --enable-unit-tests
make
make check
@endcode

The configure option is required. All of @c test/unit is inside
@c "if ENABLE_UNIT_TESTS". Without the option, @c "make check" has nothing to
build, runs no test, and succeeds. An installed CMocka alone does not enable
the tests.

@subsection unit_tests_check_vs_test "make check" is not "make test"

The two targets have similar names, but they do different things. Only one of
them can fail.

<table>
<tr><th>Target</th><th>What it does</th><th>Fails the build?</th></tr>
<tr><td>@c "make check"</td>
    <td>Builds and runs the CMocka tests.</td>
    <td>Yes. A failing test exits with a non-zero status. Use this target to
        check a change.</td></tr>
<tr><td>@c "make test"</td>
    <td>Encodes @c testcase.wav and counts the bytes that differ from the
        committed @c testcase.mp3.</td>
    <td>No. It prints a number and always succeeds.</td></tr>
</table>

@c "make test" measures changes in the bitstream. Its count is usually not zero
on a different processor, compiler or optimization level. The number is only
useful to a developer who compares it with the same build before a change. To
find out whether something is broken, use @c "make check".

@subsection unit_tests_results Where the results are

@c "make check" prints one line with PASS or FAIL for each test, and a summary.
When a test fails, the details are in files that automake writes into the build
tree:

- @c test/unit/test-suite.log: the output of all failing tests. This file is in
  @c test/unit, not at the top of the build tree.
- @c test/unit/&lt;name&gt;.log: one file per test, also for the tests that
  passed. It has the CMocka message for the failing assertion, with its file and
  line.
- @c test/unit/&lt;name&gt;.trs: the result in the form that automake reads.

You can also run a single test directly. The test programs are built in
@c test/unit and take no arguments.

@subsection unit_tests_coverage Running them under coverage

Which lines the tests run is a different question from whether they pass. It
has its own harness, see @ref maintainer_coverage.

@section unit_tests_seam Testing static functions

Some of the tested functions are @c static, so other files cannot call them.
To test them, the test file includes the whole source file with @c \#include.
For example, @c test_parse_pathlen.c does @c \#include @c "parse.c". The
included file also refers to functions and variables in other files. A small
"stubs" file provides these for the linker:

- @c test_stubs.c: the frontend globals, and the console and file helpers that
  @c get_audio.c needs.
- @c parse_test_stubs.c: the console and file helpers that @c parse.c needs.
  @c parse.c defines the frontend configuration globals itself, so this file
  does not provide them.

libmp3lame provides the real @c lame_* API. Sometimes a test must see behavior
that the return value does not show, for example a parser that loops forever
instead of returning. Then the test replaces a libc function with the
@c --wrap option of the linker.

@section unit_tests_list The tests

Each test program has its own page in this group. Four examples:

- @c test_sanity.c: checks that the test harness works (@ref test_sanity.c).
- @c test_get_audio_aiff.c: two checks in @c parse_aiff_header(), for a FORM
  size below 4 and for a sample rate that does not fit into an @c int
  (@ref test_get_audio_aiff.c).
- @c test_parse_pathlen.c: the length limit for the input and output file names
  in @c set_path_arg() (@ref test_parse_pathlen.c).
- @c test_merge_argv.c: the size of the argument vector, and the count limit in
  @c merge_argv() (@ref test_merge_argv.c).
*/
